WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.643
This lesson gets much easier when momentum is treated as a whole-system quantity during a collision.

2
00:00:05.643 --> 00:00:11.286
Each object can have its own momentum, but the conservation law belongs to the total of the isolated system.

3
00:00:11.286 --> 00:00:16.929
That means the learner should not watch only one trolley.

4
00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:22.572
The correct habit is to total the momentum before the collision and total it again after the collision.

5
00:00:22.572 --> 00:00:28.215
If external forces can be ignored, those totals match.

6
00:00:28.215 --> 00:00:33.858
Keep this takeaway in view: Conservation is a system-total statement, not a one-object shortcut.

7
00:00:33.858 --> 00:00:39.501
Momentum means mass x velocity.

8
00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:45.144
Momentum describes motion with both amount of mass and direction-sensitive velocity.

9
00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:50.787
System view means total matters.

10
00:00:50.787 --> 00:00:56.430
The conservation statement belongs to the whole collision system.

11
00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.366
This visual shows momentum as something that passes through the collision story rather than disappearing.

12
00:00:59.366 --> 00:01:02.302
Before impact, the moving trolley carries the total momentum.

13
00:01:02.302 --> 00:01:05.239
After impact, if the trolleys stick together, the same total momentum is shared by the combined mass.

14
00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.175
That is why the final speed can be smaller.

15
00:01:08.175 --> 00:01:11.111
The momentum total is preserved, but more mass is now carrying it.

16
00:01:11.111 --> 00:01:14.048
Keep this takeaway in view: The total momentum survives the collision, even though the speed pattern changes.

17
00:01:14.048 --> 00:01:16.984
Before: The moving trolley carries the system momentum.

18
00:01:16.984 --> 00:01:19.920
After: The joined mass carries the same total momentum.

19
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.644
Imagine momentum as a carry score attached to moving objects.

20
00:01:25.644 --> 00:01:31.368
A larger mass or larger velocity gives a bigger score, and direction decides the sign of the score.

21
00:01:31.368 --> 00:01:37.092
During an isolated interaction, the total carry score of the whole system is preserved.

22
00:01:37.092 --> 00:01:42.816
Objects can exchange it, combine it, or share it differently, but the total remains the same.

23
00:01:42.816 --> 00:01:48.540
The analogy helps because it turns conservation into a bookkeeping idea before the formulas arrive.

24
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:54.264
Keep this takeaway in view: Treat momentum like a signed total that must still balance across the collision.

25
00:01:54.264 --> 00:01:59.988
Bigger mass means bigger score.

26
00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:05.712
More mass carrying the same velocity means more momentum.

27
00:02:05.712 --> 00:02:11.436
Bigger velocity means bigger score.

28
00:02:11.436 --> 00:02:17.160
Faster motion also increases momentum.

29
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.539
Momentum is given by mass times velocity.

30
00:02:22.539 --> 00:02:27.918
The conservation law says total momentum before equals total momentum after for an isolated system.

31
00:02:27.918 --> 00:02:33.297
When objects stick together, the final side often becomes one combined mass times one shared velocity.

32
00:02:33.297 --> 00:02:38.676
That is why these questions often feel tidy once the before-total is calculated carefully.

33
00:02:38.676 --> 00:02:44.055
Keep this takeaway in view: Use the conservation law as a before-and-after balance, not as a memory trick.

34
00:02:44.055 --> 00:02:49.434
Individual momentum: Each object contributes mass times velocity.

35
00:02:49.434 --> 00:02:54.813
Total before: Add all momenta in the system before impact.

36
00:02:54.813 --> 00:03:00.192
p = m v.

37
00:03:00.192 --> 00:03:05.571
Sigma p_before = Sigma p_after.

38
00:03:05.571 --> 00:03:10.950
Keep direction or sign visible when momentum can act in opposite directions.

39
00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:16.518
Suppose one trolley brings 10 kilogram metres per second of momentum into the collision and the second trolley is initially at rest.

40
00:03:16.518 --> 00:03:22.086
If they stick together, the final total mass is larger than the moving mass alone.

41
00:03:22.086 --> 00:03:27.653
Since the total momentum is unchanged, the shared speed has to be smaller than the original 5 metres per second.

42
00:03:27.653 --> 00:03:33.221
That expectation matters because it gives a quick sense-check for the final answer.

43
00:03:33.221 --> 00:03:38.789
Keep this takeaway in view: Predict the direction of the speed change before doing the division.

44
00:03:38.789 --> 00:03:44.357
Same total momentum means unchanged total.

45
00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:49.924
The isolated-system momentum stays constant.

46
00:03:49.924 --> 00:03:55.492
More mass after means lower shared speed.

47
00:03:55.492 --> 00:04:01.060
The total has to be carried by the combined mass.

48
00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:05.386
Try three checks.

49
00:04:05.386 --> 00:04:09.712
What two quantities make momentum?

50
00:04:09.712 --> 00:04:14.038
Mass and velocity.

51
00:04:14.038 --> 00:04:18.365
What stays the same in an isolated collision?

52
00:04:18.365 --> 00:04:22.691
Total momentum of the system.

53
00:04:22.691 --> 00:04:27.017
And if two objects stick together after the collision, why is the shared speed often smaller than the first object's original speed?

54
00:04:27.017 --> 00:04:31.343
Because the same total momentum is now shared by a larger mass.

55
00:04:31.343 --> 00:04:35.669
Those three answers are the whole lesson in compressed form.

56
00:04:35.669 --> 00:04:39.995
Keep this takeaway in view: The system-total logic is now strong enough to support the calculation.

57
00:04:39.995 --> 00:04:44.322
Momentum ingredients means mass and velocity.

58
00:04:44.322 --> 00:04:48.648
Both matter, and direction matters through velocity.

59
00:04:48.648 --> 00:04:52.974
Conserved quantity means total momentum.

60
00:04:52.974 --> 00:04:57.300
The total system value is preserved in an isolated collision.

61
00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:03.052
You are ready for the worked example now.

62
00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:08.805
A 2 kilogram trolley moving at 5 metres per second hits a 3 kilogram trolley at rest and they stick together.

63
00:05:08.805 --> 00:05:14.558
Start with the momentum before.

64
00:05:14.558 --> 00:05:20.310
The moving trolley contributes 2 times 5, which is 10 kilogram metres per second.

65
00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:26.062
The second trolley contributes zero.

66
00:05:26.062 --> 00:05:31.815
So the total before is 10.

67
00:05:31.815 --> 00:05:37.568
After the collision the total mass is 5 kilograms and the total momentum must still be 10.

68
00:05:37.568 --> 00:05:43.320
So write 10 equals 5 v.

69
00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:49.072
Solving gives v equals 2 metres per second.

70
00:05:49.072 --> 00:05:54.825
That answer makes physical sense because more mass is now sharing the same total momentum.

71
00:05:54.825 --> 00:06:00.578
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: conserve the total momentum and solve for the shared speed.

72
00:06:00.578 --> 00:06:06.330
Step 1: Calculate the total momentum before the collision.

73
00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:12.082
Step 2: Write the final momentum using combined mass times shared velocity.

74
00:06:12.082 --> 00:06:17.835
p_before = 2 x 5 + 3 x 0 = 10 kg m/s.

75
00:06:17.835 --> 00:06:23.588
p_after = (2 + 3) v = 5v.

76
00:06:23.588 --> 00:06:29.340
State why the final speed should be lower before you solve.
